---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Mermaid from '../../../components/Mermaid.astro';
import { tlsr8266Parts, tlsr8266PartHref } from '../../../data/series';
---

<Base title="Reverse Engineering the TLSR8266: From Vendor Blobs to LLVM and Rust">
	<p><a href="/">← back to home</a></p>

	<h1>Reverse Engineering the TLSR8266: From Vendor Blobs to LLVM and Rust</h1>
	<div style="margin: 0.75rem 0 1.5rem">
		<span class="badge live">● live in production</span>
		<span class="badge">rust</span>
		<span class="badge">llvm</span>
		<span class="badge">tc32</span>
		<span class="badge">ble mesh</span>
		<span class="badge">reverse engineering</span>
		<span class="badge">rp2040</span>
	</div>

	<p class="tagline">
		Literally all of this exists because I wanted my smart lights to smoothly tween between
		colour temperatures at dawn and dusk. How a frustration with abrupt stepping and a flaky vendor
		Wi-Fi bridge spiralled into an overclocked RP2040 programmer, Ghidra decompilation of
		proprietary Telink blobs, an LLVM backend fork, and a pure Rust firmware.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the final architecture</h2>
	<Mermaid>{`flowchart TB
		subgraph toolchain["LLVM & Rust Pipeline"]
			direction LR
			RUST["Pure Rust Crates<br/>(no_std, embedded-hal)"] -->|"cargo build<br/>--emit=llvm-ir"| BC["LLVM Bitcode (.bc)"]
			BC -->|"llvm-link + opt<br/>double-ipsccp + deadargelim"| OPT["Whole-Module IR (.bc)"]
			OPT -->|"Native TC32 llc<br/>TC32IRFixupPass"| ASM["TC32 Assembly (.s)"]
			ASM -->|"tc32-elf-as + tc32-elf-ld<br/>--gc-sections"| BIN["Firmware Binary (.bin)<br/>(77.7 KB)"]
		end

		subgraph hardware["Physical Hardware & Mesh"]
			direction LR
			DEV["E104-BT05-TB<br/>Mesh Bridge Node"] <-->|"44-byte UART Packets<br/>115200 8N1 (CRC16)"| DAEMON["Smart Manager Daemon<br/>(Rust / MQTT)"]
			DEV <-->|"BLE 1Mbps GFSK<br/>2.4GHz AES-CCM Mesh"| BULB1["Smart Light #1<br/>(TYBT1 / TLSR8266)"]
			BULB1 <-->|"Multi-hop Mesh Flood"| BULB2["Smart Light #2<br/>(TYBT1 / TLSR8266)"]
		end

		toolchain ==>|"SWire (RP2040) / BLE OTA / UART OTA"| hardware`}</Mermaid>

	<p>
		Certain Tuya BLE smart downlights and bulbs use the TYBT1 module, housing the Telink TLSR8266:
		an obscure 32-bit RISC SoC built around Telink's proprietary "TC32" instruction set. Out of the box,
		the vendor setup was annoying: a cheap USB Wi-Fi/BLE bridge that dropped packets, only 100
		brightness steps and 255 colour increments, and zero on-device interpolation. When the central
		controller sent sub-step updates during a 30-minute sunset fade, the downlights visibly stepped and
		strobed hard enough that other people in the house noticed.
	</p>
	<p>
		Today, every bulb in my house runs firmware written in Rust, compiled via a custom
		LLVM fork, running a cooperative async runtime with 32,767 internal levels of easing,
		10,455-tick PWM at 3.06 kHz, and zero vendor code.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the series</h2>
	<ul class="series-list">
		{tlsr8266Parts.map((p) => (
			<li>
				<span class="series-n">part {p.n}</span>
				<a href={tlsr8266PartHref(p)}>{p.title}</a>
				<span class="series-blurb">{p.blurb}</span>
			</li>
		))}
	</ul>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>repositories & code</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<a href="https://github.com/retsimx/tlsr8266_mesh">retsimx/tlsr8266_mesh</a> — the
			Rust BLE mesh firmware, hardware drivers, async executor, and Python flashing/OTA tools
		</li>
		<li>
			<a href="https://github.com/retsimx/llvm-project/tree/tc32">retsimx/llvm-project (tc32 branch)</a> — the
			forked LLVM toolchain adding native TC32 assembly emission, per-symbol section injection, and IR intrinsic transformations
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>standing on the shoulders of</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<a href="https://rbaron.net/blog/2021/07/06/Reverse-engineering-the-M6-smart-fitness-band.html">Raphael Baron's M6 reverse-engineering work</a> (<a href="https://github.com/rbaron/m6-reveng">rbaron/m6-reveng</a>) —
			the pioneering analysis of the Telink TLSR8266 hardware, SWS protocol, and memory architecture
		</li>
		<li>
			<a href="https://github.com/trust1995/Ghidra_TELink_TC32">trust1995/Ghidra_TELink_TC32</a> —
			the Ghidra processor definition for the Telink TC32 architecture that made decompiling the proprietary binary blobs possible
		</li>
	</ul>

